   "author": "Hollowmere team (wilderness round 4)",
   "story": "Kettle Creek was a backcountry district station at the end of the forest road: an office and a radio room, a bunkhouse for the fire crew, an engine bay for the brush truck and a steel fire-weather tower in the compound where somebody read the instruments at eight and four every day of the season. When the Cordon closed the valley it made the station Checkpoint 11: District Ranger Ines Calloway and her crew lowered the barrier across the road, checked every car against the Cordon's list and held anyone with a fever in a screening shelter for a medical team that never came. On the twelfth day the roads closed for good and the soldiers did not come back. The crew stayed. Dale turned in the engine bay and they barricaded the hall door on him; the families held at the barrier turned on their cots; the crew slept in the bunkhouse with their boots on. Ines kept the radio schedule and kept reading the weather, and on the night of the first Hum she wrote down a wind that was not there, hung the tower key in the radio room, bolted the bay and climbed to the cab with the radio and the district key. Her lamp still burns up there.",
